#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107053 :tutorial/content "The **area of a triangle** can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a *base* of the triangle and $h$ is the corresponding *height*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the area of the triangle with base \n$b = 4$\nand the corresponding height is\n$h = 8,$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84de11aac8e9f33a459f2a83647dac43eb2054445d842ac769511711366a15ec.png)\n\nThe area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At the end of the lesson, we'll demonstrate where the formula comes from. But first, let's get some practice using it." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf61df49-32c4-50ee-a6f4-21f9da391a02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107055 :tutorial/content "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where the formula comes from, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/02/02c1ace5f18e9204c1559f08f591ecd692f00691c48cbee81bc4426c3b34400e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around $\\triangle{ABC},$ as follows:\n\n![](images/89/893026324410235de9cb7ac286867006593a451556587c60219fe4375a4218a4.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nSo, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is exactly half of the area of the rectangle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{ABC} = \\dfrac{AB \\cdot CH}{2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5be18d9-4833-50c8-8800-085d64e21ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2311 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula"}]}
